Ah, you're asking about the NullPointerException, huh? Well, it's a bit like when you're trying to play a game, but you're missing a piece of the game board. You're trying to move your character, but you don't have any pieces to move on.

In programming, a null pointer exception is like that. It's when you try to access or manipulate a variable that doesn't have any value or nothing at all. It's like trying to play a game without any pieces to move on.

For example, let's say you're trying to access a variable called "myString" in your program, but you don't know what it's supposed to be. You try to access it, but you get a NullPointerException.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
String myString = null;
System.out.println(myString); // prints "null"
```

In this case, you're trying to print "myString" because you don't know what it's supposed to be.

To fix this, you need to make sure that the variable is not null before you try to access it. You can do this by checking if the variable is null before trying to access it:

```java
String myString = null;
if (myString != null) {
    System.out.println(myString); // prints "myString"
} else {
    System.out.println("myString is null"); // prints "myString is null"
}
```

So, to sum it up, a NullPointerException is like missing a piece of the game board, and it's a really bad thing that can happen in programming.

